Create a PR whose description explains the problem being solved, not the files changed. Base: **{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}**; remote: `{{OWNER_REPO}}`.

## 1. Branch state

Confirm you are on a feature branch; extract the ticket ref from its name (`<type>/{{TICKET_REF_PATTERN}}-<slug>`). Uncommitted changes: commit them first per `rules/git-workflow.md`, or ask if they look unrelated.

## 2. Intent

State the problem this PR solves in one sentence. "Add feature X" is a title, not a problem; if you cannot produce the problem sentence, re-read the ticket before writing anything else. This sentence drives the whole description.

## 3. Validate the diff

`git diff {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}...HEAD`: every changed file serves the stated intent. Anything that does not: remove it, or explain it explicitly in the description. See `instructions/description-format.md` and the `examples/` for the quality bar.

## 4. Build the description

Fill `.github/pull_request_template.md`: problem first, then approach (why this way), then concrete verification steps. Ticket link: `{{PR_TICKET_LINK_FORMAT}}`.
<!-- e.g. "Closes #<n>" for GitHub; for Linear/Jira keep the ID in the title + a Refs line in the body. -->

## 5. Create

```bash
BYPASS_CREATE_PR_HOOK=1 gh pr create --base {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} --title "<title>" --body "<body>"
```

Title states the change in ≤ ~70 chars{{TITLE_TICKET_NOTE}}<!-- Linear/Jira: " and keeps the ticket ID for auto-linking" -->. Print the PR URL.
